---
import SiteFooter from '../../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import '../../styles/global.css';

const findings = [
  {
    title: 'The form hides its requirements',
    problem: 'Required documents and accepted formats appear only after an upload fails.',
    fix: 'Show requirements before the visitor starts.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Errors arrive without a recovery path',
    problem: 'A red border signals a problem but does not explain what changed or how to continue.',
    fix: 'Name the error and place guidance beside the field.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Progress is invisible',
    problem: 'Visitors cannot tell how much work remains or whether they can return later.',
    fix: 'Show scope, progress, and save behavior from the start.',
  },
];

const repairs = [
  ['Before starting', 'Explain the outcome, documents, time, and cost.'],
  ['While completing', 'Use familiar labels, useful defaults, and visible progress.'],
  ['When errors happen', 'Preserve input and explain exactly how to recover.'],
  ['After submission', 'Confirm what happened and what the visitor should expect next.'],
];

const sectionNumber = 'm-0 mb-2 font-sans text-[0.68rem] font-bold tracking-[0.12em] text-accent';
const sectionHeading =
  'mb-6 font-display text-[clamp(2.35rem,4vw,3.7rem)] leading-[1.05] font-medium tracking-[-0.04em]';
const bodyParagraph = 'mb-5 text-pretty';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="A demonstration critique showing how a confusing online form can be made clearer, more accessible, and easier to complete."
    />
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml" />
    <title>
      What a broken form teaches us about usable design | Rwanda Free Space
    </title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <a
      class="fixed top-3 left-3 z-50 -translate-y-[160%] bg-ink px-4 py-2.5 text-canvas focus:translate-y-0"
      href="#article-content">Skip to article</a
    >
    <SiteHeader />

    <main
      class="mx-auto w-full max-w-[90rem] px-[clamp(1.25rem,4vw,4.5rem)] max-sm:px-0"
      id="article-content"
    >
      <header
        class="mx-auto max-w-[76rem] py-[clamp(2.5rem,5vw,4.5rem)] pb-[clamp(3.5rem,6vw,5rem)] max-md:pt-12 max-sm:px-[clamp(1.25rem,4vw,4.5rem)]"
      >
        <a
          class="mb-[clamp(2.5rem,4vw,3.75rem)] inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-[0.78rem] font-bold text-muted no-underline hover:text-accent"
          href="/#stories"
        >
          <svg
            class="size-4 fill-none stroke-current [stroke-linecap:round] [stroke-linejoin:round]"
            viewBox="0 0 16 16"
            aria-hidden="true"><path d="M13 8H3m4-4L3 8l4 4"></path></svg
          >
          All critiques
        </a>

        <div
          class="grid grid-cols-[minmax(0,1.25fr)_minmax(18rem,.75fr)] items-end gap-[clamp(3rem,7vw,7rem)] max-md:grid-cols-1 max-md:gap-10"
        >
          <div>
            <p
              class="m-0 mb-5 text-[0.72rem] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-accent"
            >
              Demonstration critique · UX teardown
            </p>
            <h1
              class="m-0 max-w-[15ch] font-display text-[clamp(3.5rem,4.8vw,5.35rem)] leading-[.98] font-medium tracking-[-0.05em] text-pretty max-md:text-[clamp(3rem,8vw,4.25rem)] max-sm:text-[clamp(2.65rem,11vw,3.45rem)]"
            >
              What a broken public-service form teaches us about usable design
            </h1>
          </div>

          <div
            class="border-t-[3px] border-accent pt-6 max-md:grid max-md:grid-cols-[1.2fr_.8fr] max-md:gap-8 max-sm:block"
          >
            <p
              class="m-0 mb-8 max-w-[31rem] text-[clamp(1.02rem,1.35vw,1.18rem)] leading-[1.65] text-muted text-pretty max-md:mb-0 max-sm:mb-8"
            >
              A form can technically work and still fail the people using it.
              This test article examines five common points of friction and
              turns each one into a practical design improvement.
            </p>
            <div
              class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-x-6 gap-y-4 border-t border-line pt-5 max-md:border-0 max-md:pt-0 max-sm:border-t max-sm:pt-5"
            >
              <p class="col-span-2 m-0 text-xs font-semibold">
                <span
                  class="mb-0.5 block text-[0.65rem] font-semibold tracking-[0.08em] text-muted uppercase"
                  >Written by</span
                > Rwanda Free Space
              </p>
              <p class="m-0 text-xs font-semibold">
                <span
                  class="mb-0.5 block text-[0.65rem] font-semibold tracking-[0.08em] text-muted uppercase"
                  >Published</span
                >
                <time datetime="2026-08-30">30 August 2026</time>
              </p>
              <p class="m-0 text-xs font-semibold">
                <span
                  class="mb-0.5 block text-[0.65rem] font-semibold tracking-[0.08em] text-muted uppercase"
                  >Reading time</span
                > 8 minutes
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </header>

      <figure class="mx-auto mb-[clamp(4rem,8vw,7rem)] max-w-[76rem]">
        <div
          class="relative grid min-h-[clamp(25rem,48vw,38rem)] place-items-center overflow-hidden bg-accent max-sm:min-h-[25rem]"
          aria-hidden="true"
        >
          <span
            class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 bg-[radial-gradient(rgba(255,255,255,.16)_0.7px,transparent_0.7px)] bg-[length:5px_5px] opacity-30"
          ></span>
          <div
            class="absolute inset-x-0 top-0 flex gap-2 border-b border-white/35 p-4"
          >
            <span class="size-2 rounded-full border border-[#fffaf4]"
            ></span><span class="size-2 rounded-full border border-[#fffaf4]"
            ></span><span class="size-2 rounded-full border border-[#fffaf4]"
            ></span>
          </div>

          <div
            class="relative z-10 flex w-[min(78%,36rem)] flex-col bg-canvas p-[clamp(2rem,5vw,4rem)] shadow-[1.1rem_1.1rem_0_#17221d] max-sm:w-[78%] max-sm:px-6 max-sm:py-8 max-sm:shadow-[.65rem_.65rem_0_#17221d]"
          >
            <span class="mb-4 block h-5 w-[58%] bg-ink"></span>
            <span class="mb-8 block h-2 w-[78%] bg-line"></span>
            <span class="mb-5 block h-14 w-full border-2 border-accent"></span>
            <span class="-mt-3 mb-6 block h-2 w-[52%] bg-accent"></span>
            <span class="mb-5 block h-14 w-full border border-muted"></span>
            <span class="mt-2 block h-11 w-[36%] bg-ink"></span>
          </div>

          <span
            class="absolute top-1/4 left-[17%] z-20 grid size-11 place-items-center rounded-full border-2 border-ink bg-[#fffaf4] font-display text-xl font-bold max-sm:size-9 max-sm:text-base"
            >1</span
          >
          <span
            class="absolute top-[48%] right-[16%] z-20 grid size-11 place-items-center rounded-full border-2 border-ink bg-[#fffaf4] font-display text-xl font-bold max-sm:size-9 max-sm:text-base"
            >2</span
          >
          <span
            class="absolute bottom-[16%] left-[21%] z-20 grid size-11 place-items-center rounded-full border-2 border-ink bg-[#fffaf4] font-display text-xl font-bold max-sm:size-9 max-sm:text-base"
            >3</span
          >
        </div>
        <figcaption class="mt-3 text-xs text-muted">
          A representative form used to demonstrate recurring interface
          problems.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div
        class="grid grid-cols-[15rem_minmax(0,45rem)] justify-center gap-[clamp(3rem,8vw,8rem)] pb-[clamp(5rem,10vw,9rem)] max-md:grid-cols-1 max-md:gap-14 max-sm:px-[clamp(1.25rem,4vw,4.5rem)]"
      >
        <aside
          class="sticky top-8 self-start max-md:static"
          aria-label="Article summary"
        >
          <p
            class="m-0 mb-4 text-[0.68rem] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-accent"
          >
            In this critique
          </p>
          <ol
            class="m-0 list-none border-t border-line p-0 [counter-reset:article-section] max-md:grid max-md:grid-cols-2 max-sm:grid-cols-1"
          >
            {
              [
                ["problem", "The core problem"],
                ["friction", "Where the form creates friction"],
                ["repair", "A better approach"],
                ["standard", "The wider standard"],
              ].map(([anchor, label], index) => (
                <li
                  class:list={[
                    "border-b border-line",
                    index % 2 === 0 && "max-md:border-r max-sm:border-r-0",
                  ]}
                >
                  <a
                    class="grid grid-cols-[1.6rem_1fr] gap-2.5 py-3.5 text-xs leading-snug font-semibold no-underline hover:text-accent max-md:px-3"
                    href={`#${anchor}`}
                  >
                    <span class="text-[0.62rem] text-muted">0{index + 1}</span>
                    {label}
                  </a>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ol>
          <p
            class="mt-6 border-l-2 border-accent pl-4 text-xs leading-relaxed text-muted"
          >
            <strong class="mb-1 block text-ink">Testing note</strong>This is
            representative sample content. It does not describe a named
            organization or live service.
          </p>
        </aside>

        <article
          class="min-w-0 font-display text-[clamp(1.16rem,1.4vw,1.3rem)] leading-[1.78]"
        >
          <p
            class="m-0 mb-16 border-b border-line pb-10 text-[clamp(1.4rem,2.2vw,1.85rem)] leading-normal text-pretty"
          >
            The worst forms rarely look completely broken. They load. Their
            inputs accept text. A button eventually sends something somewhere.
            The failure appears in the gap between technical completion and
            human understanding.
          </p>

          <section class="scroll-mt-8" id="problem">
            <p class={sectionNumber}>01</p>
            <h2 class={sectionHeading}>The core problem</h2>
            <p class={bodyParagraph}>
              People arrive at a public-service form with a specific goal. They
              may need a document, an appointment, or permission to continue an
              important process. The interface should reduce uncertainty. Too
              often it adds more.
            </p>
            <p class={bodyParagraph}>
              A weak form asks for unfamiliar information without explaining
              why, marks errors only after submission, and clears useful work
              when something goes wrong. None of these failures is visually
              dramatic. Together, they make the service feel unreliable.
            </p>
            <blockquote
              class="my-12 border-y border-l-4 border-y-line border-l-accent py-8 pl-[clamp(1.5rem,4vw,3rem)]"
            >
              <p
                class="m-0 max-w-[22ch] text-[clamp(1.9rem,4vw,3.2rem)] leading-[1.12] tracking-[-0.035em]"
              >
                A working submit button is not the same as a usable service.
              </p>
            </blockquote>
          </section>

          <section
            class="mt-[clamp(4.5rem,8vw,7rem)] scroll-mt-8"
            id="friction"
          >
            <p class={sectionNumber}>02</p>
            <h2 class={sectionHeading}>Where the form creates friction</h2>
            <p class={bodyParagraph}>
              The first issue is language. Labels written for the database or
              internal office process force the visitor to translate the system
              before answering. Plain labels, short examples, and a sentence
              explaining unusual requests can remove that burden.
            </p>

            {
              findings.map((finding, index) => (
                <div class="mt-10 grid grid-cols-[2.2rem_minmax(0,1fr)] gap-x-5 border-t border-line py-6 max-sm:grid-cols-[1.8rem_minmax(0,1fr)] max-sm:gap-x-3">
                  <span class="font-sans text-[0.68rem] font-bold text-accent">
                    0{index + 1}
                  </span>
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="mb-1 font-display text-[1.35rem] leading-tight font-medium">
                      {finding.title}
                    </h3>
                    <p class="m-0 text-base leading-relaxed text-muted">
                      {finding.problem}
                    </p>
                  </div>
                  <p class="col-start-2 mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-ink">
                    <span class="mr-3 font-sans text-[0.65rem] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.08em] text-accent">
                      Fix
                    </span>
                    {finding.fix}
                  </p>
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </section>

          <section class="mt-[clamp(4.5rem,8vw,7rem)] scroll-mt-8" id="repair">
            <p class={sectionNumber}>03</p>
            <h2 class={sectionHeading}>A better approach</h2>
            <p class={bodyParagraph}>
              Start by writing the journey in the visitor's language. State the
              outcome, list what is needed, and divide the form only where the
              steps match a real change in the task. Keep completed information
              safe when validation or the network fails.
            </p>
            <div class="mt-10 border-t border-line">
              {
                repairs.map(([label, copy]) => (
                  <p class="m-0 grid grid-cols-[minmax(8rem,.7fr)_1.3fr] gap-6 border-b border-line py-4 text-base leading-normal max-sm:grid-cols-1 max-sm:gap-1.5">
                    <span class="font-sans text-[0.68rem] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.05em] text-accent">
                      {label}
                    </span>
                    {copy}
                  </p>
                ))
              }
            </div>
          </section>

          <section
            class="mt-[clamp(4.5rem,8vw,7rem)] scroll-mt-8"
            id="standard"
          >
            <p class={sectionNumber}>04</p>
            <h2 class={sectionHeading}>The wider standard</h2>
            <p class={bodyParagraph}>
              Better forms do more than improve completion rates. They
              communicate respect. They tell people that their time, data, and
              attention matter. That should be the baseline for every digital
              service built for the public.
            </p>
            <p class={bodyParagraph}>
              The useful question is not whether the page shipped. It is whether
              someone can understand it, complete it, recover from a mistake,
              and trust what happens next.
            </p>
          </section>

          <footer
            class="mt-[clamp(5rem,10vw,8rem)] bg-surface p-[clamp(2rem,5vw,4rem)]"
          >
            <p
              class="m-0 mb-5 font-sans text-[0.72rem] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-accent"
            >
              End of demonstration critique
            </p>
            <h2
              class="mb-8 max-w-[12ch] font-display text-[clamp(2rem,4vw,3.4rem)] leading-[1.05] font-medium tracking-[-0.04em]"
            >
              Good criticism should leave a path forward.
            </h2>
            <a
              class="flex w-fit items-center gap-2 border-b border-accent py-3 font-sans text-[0.84rem] font-bold text-accent-dark no-underline"
              href="/#stories"
            >
              Return to all critiques
              <svg
                class="size-4 fill-none stroke-current [stroke-linecap:round] [stroke-linejoin:round]"
                viewBox="0 0 16 16"
                aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 8h10M9 4l4 4-4 4"></path></svg
              >
            </a>
          </footer>
        </article>
      </div>
    </main>

    <SiteFooter />
  </body>
</html>
